e:["$","p",null,{"children":["That exit code is a fixed sentinel: every signal collapses to 9, so you cannot\ntell SIGKILL from SIGTERM from the code alone. The ",["$","code",null,{"children":"warn!"}]," line above is the\nonly thing that tells you it was a signal at all. From Bazel's side this looks\nlike an ordinary action failure with a non-zero exit status, so ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Bazel will\nnot treat it as an infrastructure error and will not retry it"}],". On the\nscheduler it lands in\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge_execution_completed_count_total{execution_result=\"failure\"}"}]," like\nany other non-zero exit; there is no exported metric that singles out\nsignal deaths, so the log line is what you alert on."]}]
f:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The worker process itself was killed."}]," The scheduler notices after\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"worker_timeout_s"}]," (default 10 seconds, checked once a second), evicts it, and\nre-queues its in-flight actions. A lost worker does not spend the action's\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"max_job_retries"}],"; it has its own budget, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"max_worker_loss_retries"}]," (default\n10), so after that many losses the client finally sees, as a\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"FAILED_PRECONDITION"}]," that Bazel does not resubmit:"]}]
